---
title: Automatic differentiation over frames
summary: How a straight-line program is differentiated by composing each step's derivative maps over its computation graph, forward with frames of tangents, backwards over its backpropagation graph with frames of cotangents, and forward-over-reverse for Hessian products.
---

<script>
	import Statement from '$lib/components/Statement.svelte';
	import Proof from '$lib/components/Proof.svelte';
	import ModelGraph from '$lib/components/ModelGraph.svelte';
	import * as forwardOverReverse from './statements/forward-over-reverse.md';
	import { computation, backpropagation } from './program.graph';
</script>

Automatic differentiation computes the derivatives of a program exactly, up to rounding, from the
derivatives of the steps it is made of. This note states the three ways of composing them that a
library needs (pushing tangents forward, pulling cotangents back, and differentiating the pull-back
once more for second order), each over a *frame* of several directions at once.

## Programs and their computation graphs

A *straight-line program* has slots $s_0, s_1, \dots$, each holding a vector, and runs a sequence of
steps

$$
	s_{o_k} \leftarrow \map_k\bigl(s_{i_{k,1}}, \dots, s_{i_{k,m_k}}\bigr), @tag(step)
$$

each writing one slot from slots written earlier. Each step's map $\map_k$ is smooth, with partial
Jacobians $\partial_j \map_k$, one per input, of shape (output dimension) by (dimension of input
$j$). When an input is not differentiated, its partial Jacobian is simply never used.

A program is pictured by its *computation graph*. Its nodes are the slots, and it has an edge from
each input of a step to that step's output, so a node with incoming edges is computed by the step
that writes it, from the nodes those edges come from. Because each step reads only slots written
before it, the computation graph has no cycles, and the order of the steps is one of its
topological orders. Its *roots*, the nodes with no incoming edge and so no ancestor, are the
slots no step writes: the program's inputs, the variables $\point$ we differentiate with respect
to. Some nodes are
*targets*, the values to differentiate, $f(\point)$. A node with several outgoing edges is a slot
read by several steps.

<ModelGraph spec={computation} />

A *frame* is a matrix whose columns are directions: a tangent frame at a slot of dimension
$\dimension$ is a $\dimension \times n$ matrix $\tangent$ of $n$ tangent vectors, and a cotangent
frame a $\dimension \times n$ matrix $\alpha$ whose columns are linear functionals, written as
columns. Below, $\tangent_s$ and $\alpha_s$ are the frames held at slot $s$.

## Forward: pushing tangent frames

Seed each root with a tangent frame $\tangent_{\point}$ (missing means zero), and run the steps in
order, each pushing its inputs' frames forward along the computation graph's edges by its
*Jacobian–vector product*:

$$
	\tangent_{s_{o_k}} = \sum_j \partial_j \map_k \; \tangent_{s_{i_{k,j}}} . @tag(jvp)
$$

By the chain rule, the frame reaching a target is $\sum_l \partial f / \partial \point_l \cdot \tangent_{\point_l}$:
one pass with a frame of $n$ columns gives $n$ directional derivatives, and seeding the columns of
an identity gives the whole Jacobian.

## Reverse: pulling cotangent frames over the backpropagation graph

The reverse pass runs on the *backpropagation graph*: the same nodes as the computation graph, with
every edge reversed and labelled by the pull-back $\partial_j \map_k\transpose$ of the step it came
from. Since reversing the edges swaps incoming for outgoing, a node is a root of the computation
graph if and only if it is a *leaf* of the backpropagation graph, a node with no outgoing edge.
Cotangents enter at the targets, flow along its edges, and end at those leaves, the program's
inputs. A node with several incoming edges, a slot read by several steps, receives a cotangent
along each, and holds their sum.

<ModelGraph spec={backpropagation} />

Seed each target with a cotangent frame $\alpha_f$, every other slot with zero, and run the steps
backwards, each adding its *vector–Jacobian product*, the pull-back of its output's cotangent, into
each input:

$$
	\alpha_{s_{i_{k,j}}} \mathrel{+}= \partial_j \map_k\transpose \, \alpha_{s_{o_k}} . @tag(vjp)
$$

The accumulation, which sums the cotangents arriving along every incoming edge of the
backpropagation graph, is the chain rule for a slot with several consumers. When the steps are done,
each root holds $\alpha_{\point_l} = \sum_f (\partial f / \partial \point_l)\transpose \alpha_f$. For a scalar
target seeded with $\alpha_f = 1$, that is the gradient $\nabla_{\point_l} f$, whatever the number
of roots.

Each column of a frame costs a small multiple of one evaluation of the program, in either
direction. A Jacobian with few rows is cheaper in reverse, one with few columns forward.

## Forward-over-reverse: Hessian products

For second derivatives, differentiate the reverse pass itself along a path $\point + t\tangent$. Run
the forward pass of [](eq:jvp) with velocities $\tangent$ seeded at the roots, so that every slot
holds its value $s$ and its velocity $\tangent_s$, then run the reverse pass over the
backpropagation graph carrying two cotangents per slot: $\alpha_s$, as in [](eq:vjp), and
$\beta_s$, its derivative along the path. One column of $\tangent$ is one path; a frame of $n$
columns runs $n$ paths at once, and then $\beta_s$ is a frame of $n$ columns while $\alpha_s$ is the
same for all of them. (A cotangent frame of several columns together with a velocity frame of
several columns would make $\beta$ a three-way array; the passes here take one cotangent column.)

Differentiating [](eq:vjp) along the path by the product rule gives

$$
	\beta_{s_{i_{k,j}}} \mathrel{+}= \hvp_{k,j}(\alpha_{s_{o_k}}) + \partial_j \map_k\transpose \, \beta_{s_{o_k}},
	\qquad
	\hvp_{k,j}(\alpha) \defeq \frac{\dd}{\dd t}\Big|_{t=0} \partial_j \map_k(s + t\tangent_s)\transpose \alpha
	= \sum_l \bigl(\partial_l \partial_j \map_k\,[\tangent_{s_{i_{k,l}}}]\bigr)\transpose \alpha . @tag(leibniz)
$$

The first term is the step's own second-order map, which depends on the velocities of its inputs;
the second is the step's ordinary pull-back, applied to $\beta$. A library writes the first for
each step and the program applies the rule. Two consequences are useful when writing $\hvp$ for a
step. If a step is linear, its partial Jacobians are constant and its $\hvp$ is zero. If the
pull-back into input $j$ does not involve input $l$, then the velocity of input $l$ contributes
nothing to $\hvp_{k,j}$.

<Statement {...forwardOverReverse} />

<Proof>

The cotangents $\alpha_s$ are functions of the roots: the reverse recurrence holds as an identity
in $\point$, with every $\partial_j \map_k$ evaluated at the slot values the program computes from
$\point$. Differentiate that identity along $\point + t\tangent$ for one column $\tangent$. By the
product rule, $\beta_s \defeq \frac{\dd}{\dd t}\alpha_s(\point + t\tangent)\big|_{t=0}$ satisfies
[](eq:leibniz), because the slot values move with the velocities the forward pass computed, and the
seeds $\alpha_f = 1$ and $\beta_f = 0$ are the value and the derivative of a constant. At a root,
$\alpha_{\point_i}(\point) = \nabla_{\point_i} f(\point)$ identically in $\point$, so its derivative
along the path is $\sum_l \nabla_{\point_i} \nabla_{\point_l} f(\point)\,\tangent_{\point_l}$.

</Proof>

With all roots stacked into one vector, $\beta_\point = \nabla^2 f(\point)\,\tangent$: a frame of
$n$ velocities gives $n$ columns of the Hessian for about $n$ times the cost of a gradient, and the
identity frame gives all of it.
